The '''2022 North Tyneside Metropolitan Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022. One third of councillors—20 out of 60—on [[North Tyneside Metropolitan Borough Council]] were elected. The election took place alongside [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections across the United Kingdom]].

In the [[2021 North Tyneside Metropolitan Borough Council election|previous council election in 2021]], the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] maintained its control of the council, holding 50 seats after the election, with nine other councillors from the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]].

== Background ==
[[File:North Tyneside UK local election 2021 map.svg|thumb|Result of the 2021 council election]]
The [[Local Government Act 1972]] created a two-tier system of metropolitan counties and districts covering [[Greater Manchester]], [[Merseyside]], [[South Yorkshire]], [[Tyne and Wear]], the [[West Midlands (county)|West Midlands]], and [[West Yorkshire]] starting in 1974. North Tyneside was a district of the Tyne and Wear metropolitan county.<ref name=guide>{{cite book |title=Local Government in England and Wales: A Guide to the New System |year=1974 |publisher=[[HMSO]] |location=London |isbn=0-11-750847-0 |page=7 }}</ref> The [[Local Government Act 1985]] abolished the metropolitan counties, with metropolitan districts taking on most of their powers as [[metropolitan borough]]s. The [[North of Tyne Combined Authority]] was created in 2018 and began electing the [[mayor of the North of Tyne]] from 2019, which was given strategic powers covering a region covering some of the same area as the former Tyne and Wear metropolitan county, as well as Northumberland.<ref>{{cite web |last=Walker |first=Jonathan |date=2018-11-02 |title=It's official: New North of Tyne Combined Authority is launched |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/a-golden-era-north-east-15362971 |access-date=2022-03-30 |website=ChronicleLive |language=en}}</ref>

Since its creation, North Tyneside has generally been under [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] control, with some periods of [[no overall control]] and [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] control from 2008 to 2013. Labour has had an overall majority of seats on the council since the [[2011 North Tyneside Metropolitan Borough Council election|2011 election]], when the party gained seats. In the [[2021 North Tyneside Metropolitan Borough Council election|most recent council election in 2021]], Labour won eighteen seats with 48.6% of the vote to hold 50 overall, while the Conservatives won five seats with 35.7% of the vote to hold nine seats overall. The [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] received 7.1% of the vote and the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] received 6.2% of the vote but neither party won any seats.<ref>{{cite web |last=Soden |first=Herbert |date=2021-05-07 |title=North Tyneside Council local elections results in full |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/full-list-candidates-standing-north-20385101 |access-date=2022-03-30 |website=ChronicleLive |language=en}}</ref> North Tyneside has had a [[mayor of North Tyneside|single authority mayor]] since 2001, a position which has been held by Labour and Conservative politicians. Most recently, [[Norma Redfearn]] has been the Labour mayor of North Tyneside since 2013, and she was last re-elected in 2021.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2021-05-08 |title=Election 2021: Norma Redfearn re-elected North Tyneside Mayor |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-tyne-57038817 |access-date=2022-03-30}}</ref>

The positions up for election in 2021 were last [[2018 North Tyneside Metropolitan Borough Council election|elected in 2018]]. In that election, Labour won eighteen seats on 56.8% of the vote while the Conservatives won two seats with 31.8% of the vote. The Liberal Democrats received 5.9% of the vote but didn't win any seats.<ref>{{cite web |last=Seddon |first=Sean |date=2018-05-04 |title=North Tyneside local elections 2018 - results in full |url=http://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/local-election-north-tyneside---9146996 |access-date=2022-03-30 |website=ChronicleLive |language=en}}</ref>

== Electoral process ==
The council elects its councillors in thirds, with a third being up for election every year for three years, with no election in the fourth year.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.gov.uk/guidance/local-government-structure-and-elections#electoral-cycles|title=Local government structure and elections|work=GOV.UK|access-date=2018-04-27|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/694598/Election_Timetable_in_England.pdf|title=Election Timetable in England}}</ref> The election took place by [[first-past-the-post voting]], with all wards being represented by three councillors, one of whom is elected each election year to serve a four-year term.

All [[Elections in the United Kingdom#Registration procedure|registered electors]] ([[British citizen|British]], [[Irish citizen|Irish]], [[Commonwealth citizen|Commonwealth]] and [[European Union]] citizens) living in North Tyneside aged 18 or over will be entitled to vote in the election. People who live at two addresses in different councils, such as university students with different term-time and holiday addresses, are entitled to be registered for and vote in elections in both local authorities. Voting in-person at polling stations took place from 07:00 to 22:00 on election day, and voters will be able to apply for postal votes or proxy votes in advance of the election.

